    "rationale": "The only RTL attributed to uart_rx.v on this path is the cycle_counter register itself (_124902_, the startpoint DFF). Everything downstream of its Q output \u2014 the 12 unattributed cells (NOR4\u2192NAND4\u2192OAI22\u2192NOR2\u2192AOI211\u2192AND3\u2192AND3\u2192NAND3\u2192XOR2\u2192XOR2\u2192XOR2\u2192NOR3, summing to 0.86 ns of the 0.94 ns path, i.e. >90% of the delay) \u2014 is combinational logic that sits between this register and the endpoint DFFR_X1 in a completely different module (rtl/vendor/async_fifo/rtl/wptr_full.v:52). None of that logic appears in the given scope: lines 170-180 only describe the counter's own next-state increment (its D-side), which is not on this timing path at all (this path starts at cycle_counter's Q, not its D).\n\nRETIME is not available: there is no register inside the combinational chain to move \u2014 the only register we can see is the path's startpoint, and moving it would change the counter's own function/latency for every other consumer, not just this path.\n\nRESTRUCTURE is not available: we have no visibility into, and no edit scope over, the 12-cell combinational cloud that actually causes the violation. The XOR2-heavy tail (three XOR2_X1 stages plus NOR3) right before the wptr_full.v register is characteristic of gray-code encoding/comparison logic typical of async-FIFO pointer paths \u2014 that logic almost certainly lives in wptr_full.v, not in uart_rx.v.\n\nPIPELINE is not appropriate here either: inserting a register in a scope that contains none of the dominant logic would not shorten the critical chain, and blindly adding a stage on the counter's Q would ripple into every other fanout of cycle_counter without evidence that this specific reconvergent path is even balanced for it.\n\nSince the dominant delay is outside the RTL I was permitted to edit, I am declining with NO_FIX and flagging the likely real location for the fix.",
